WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:05.730
[bones cracking]

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:06.810
Antonio Webb: I love those sounds,

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.670
but other people may
cringe when they hear them.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.750
Hi, my name is Dr. Antonio Webb.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.890
I am an orthopedic spine surgeon.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.770
I've seen lots of injuries
in my 11 years of training

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.050
to become an orthopedic spine surgeon.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.870
Been in Iraq as a combat medic.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:23.970
In today's video,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.330
we're gonna be going
over some movie scenes

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.443
and reacting to some of the injuries.

00:00:32.812 --> 00:00:34.990
[Batman grunting]

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.760
Bane: Or your body!
[cracking]

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.530
Antonio: That's pretty
unlikely that that will happen

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.777
and cause a significant injury.

00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:44.670
[Batman screaming]

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.378
Prisoner: There's a vertebra

00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:48.200
protruding from your back.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.650
Antonio: Their vertebrae is actually

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.041
sticking from their back.

00:00:51.041 --> 00:00:54.120
That would be a pretty morbid injury.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.490
But in this scene here,

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.065
the chiropractor that was trying to

00:00:59.065 --> 00:01:01.989
push his vertebrae back in place,

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:04.440
that usually doesn't happen.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.800
There's a new phenomenon
called the Y strap,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.133
where they put you in traction

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.341
and they just pull on your spine,

00:01:11.341 --> 00:01:12.820
[bones cracking]

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.883
but that's usually not what happens

00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:18.443
when we're trying to push
a vertebrae back in place.

00:01:18.443 --> 00:01:21.257
When we see a bone, especially a spine,

00:01:21.257 --> 00:01:23.480
sticking out in someone's body,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.230
that patient most likely will not survive.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.730
The survival rating of this,

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:30.604
one out of 10,

00:01:30.604 --> 00:01:34.350
just because the vertebrae
that the chiropractor

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.125
saw sticking out of his back.

00:01:37.256 --> 00:01:38.342
[gunshots blasting]

00:01:38.342 --> 00:01:40.925
[John grunting and crashing]

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.610
Antonio: That's a pretty hard fall.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.090
I can't see how big of a fall that was,

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.290
but patients come in
after sustaining falls.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.760
Usually we have patients that come in,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.410
they fall from their roof,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.270
or they're up on a building
doing construction,

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.490
and then they fall.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.610
Usually they land, what's
called a FOOSH injury,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.410
fall on outstretched hands,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.050
when you're landing like this.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.130
Usually they come in
with both of their wrists

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.320
that are usually broken.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.170
Or they have calcaneus fractures,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.045
which is the heel bone.

00:02:13.045 --> 00:02:16.200
Sometimes when people land,
they landed on their feet,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.737
their heels, and they
just crush those bones.

00:02:19.737 --> 00:02:21.445
And they can have tibia fractures,

00:02:21.445 --> 00:02:23.803
which is your lower leg.

00:02:23.803 --> 00:02:26.661
We put them in what's
called external fixators

00:02:26.661 --> 00:02:29.020
to stabilize that area of the bone

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.245
until the soft tissue kind of,

00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:32.528
swelling goes down.

00:02:32.528 --> 00:02:34.990
So, he would have most likely had injuries

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:39.620
to his facial bones, mandible fracture,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.111
lots of different fractures

00:02:42.111 --> 00:02:44.470
and musculoskeletal injuries.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.130
Most likely rib fractures,
which are pretty common.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.520
You can have what's called a pneumothorax.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.880
The rib fracture will
actually puncture the lung,

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.571
requiring a chest tube,

00:02:54.571 --> 00:02:56.854
which is a tube that is put in the chest

00:02:56.854 --> 00:03:00.790
by general surgeons to reinflate the lung.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.540
The areas that he landed
on, the tops of the roofs,

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.070
the things that he hit on the way down

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.000
may have braced him some,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.300
but there's really no correct way to fall.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.550
It's probably likely,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.510
but he would have sustained
a lot of injuries.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.730
A patient getting up and walking

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.820
after something like this is pretty low.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.470
Jimmy: Holy shit, how much?

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.817
Vinny: Jimmy!
[crashing]

00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:25.940
[car horn beeping]

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.420
Antonio: That's a pretty
serious motor vehicle collision,

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:30.940
is what we call it, MVC.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.460
Some patients are actually
ejected from their vehicle.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.670
This type of car accident,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.800
where there's a head-on collision,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.566
you can have injuries to your chest,

00:03:40.566 --> 00:03:42.715
you can have injuries to your wrist,

00:03:42.715 --> 00:03:44.253
to your aorta.

00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:49.247
When there is a really fast
deceleration of a moving object,

00:03:49.247 --> 00:03:52.370
it can actually tear your great vessels,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.070
and these vessels can bleed really fast.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.830
You can actually collect 2
liters, 3 liters of blood

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.550
inside your chest cavity
within minutes, within seconds.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.380
And this is gonna be very fatal

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.490
for a lot of patients
who have these injuries.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.606
Luckily, there's airbags

00:04:10.606 --> 00:04:13.664
with the help of seat belts.

00:04:13.664 --> 00:04:18.650
A lot of injuries can
be kind of mitigated,

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.930
but this is a pretty serious accident

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.510
where patients will come
in with multiple injuries,

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.410
polytrauma is what we call it.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.110
This bone here is called
your C2 or your odontoid.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.780
Can be a pretty serious injury,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.040
especially in a young patient

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.440
where the bone does not heal.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.664
And the option is to
put screws into there,

00:04:39.664 --> 00:04:40.730
that bone there,

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.060
or to place them in a halo.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:46.495
So, these are kind of
metal rods and clamps

00:04:46.495 --> 00:04:49.656
that we use to place on the head,

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.180
and patients will actually wear this

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.320
for up to eight to 12 weeks.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.790
And sometimes these
screws will become loose

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.800
or they become infected, and
they have to be replaced.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.720
So, that's what he's doing.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.899
This can be pretty painful
for a lot of patients

00:05:02.899 --> 00:05:06.590
and uncomfortable, but
we usually anesthetize

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.310
or give them some pain medications before.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.410
And then these bars and rods are used

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.798
to stabilize the spine, the head,

00:05:13.798 --> 00:05:16.030
and then the neck until it heals.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.930
So, this is pretty realistic.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.540
But I think his survival chance

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.850
is pretty good at this point,
if he's getting a halo.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.979
Probably six out of 10.

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:27.611
[intense music]

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.750
He did have a gush of blood
that came from somewhere.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.125
I would imagine that it came from

00:05:39.125 --> 00:05:42.233
his carotid artery, maybe.

00:05:42.233 --> 00:05:44.208
You do have a vessel that's

00:05:44.208 --> 00:05:46.016
on the inside of your neck, here,

00:05:46.016 --> 00:05:50.332
that if you're stabbed or
that vessel was injured,

00:05:50.332 --> 00:05:52.720
that vessel can bleed quite a bit.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.488
And patients actually can die just from

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.157
if you don't hold pressure
kind of over that vessel,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:02.850
but this is a pretty fatal injury.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.608
Your spinal cord is actually surrounded

00:06:04.608 --> 00:06:06.230
by lots of ligaments.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.059
It's surrounded by the spinal column,

00:06:08.059 --> 00:06:10.330
which is a number of different bones

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.240
that protect the spinal cord.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.460
You actually can get stabbed
in the spinal cord itself

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.068
and sustain different,

00:06:20.068 --> 00:06:21.943
various neurological injuries

00:06:21.943 --> 00:06:23.552
depending on the location

00:06:23.552 --> 00:06:25.540
of the stab wound in the spinal cord.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.030
Is it in the ventral portion

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.310
of the spinal cord,
which is the front part?

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.010
Is it in the posterior aspect?

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.350
That will dictate what
neurologic presentation

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.760
that is seen, so.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.693
The kidneys are in the back,

00:06:40.693 --> 00:06:43.501
but a little bit lower, near the L1,

00:06:43.501 --> 00:06:46.413
L2 region. That can be injured.

00:06:46.413 --> 00:06:49.718
Your aorta is just off
to the side of the spine.

00:06:49.718 --> 00:06:52.820
If you have a injury
or stab wound to that,

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.160
that can be pretty fatal.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.570
You have to actually split the chest open

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:59.422
to actually find that
tear and try to repair it.

00:06:59.422 --> 00:07:03.435
By that time, a lot of patients will die

00:07:03.435 --> 00:07:04.950
from a injury like that.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.960
But this is a pretty fatal injury,

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.449
that the chance of him surviving this

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:12.400
without attention is probably really low.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.011
Two out of 10.

00:07:14.011 --> 00:07:16.678
[bear growling]
[Hugh screaming]

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.070
And you can see he's
laying on the ground there.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.367
This is a state of shock
that a lot of patients,

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:26.000
when they're undergoing certain types

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.025
of injuries or trauma,

00:07:28.025 --> 00:07:29.616
your body goes into a state of shock

00:07:29.616 --> 00:07:33.568
and you basically are kind of in a fog.

00:07:33.568 --> 00:07:36.310
You really don't recall
kind of what's going on.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.810
Your body is resorting
all of your blood supply,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.550
all of your energy and your nutrients

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:47.169
to the muscles and the
body parts that need them

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:50.400
for that fight-or-flight
response that people talk about.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.975
We do have other bones
and soft tissue skin

00:07:53.975 --> 00:07:56.683
that protects us from outside injuries,

00:07:56.683 --> 00:07:59.233
but you can imagine

00:07:59.233 --> 00:08:01.495
200, 300 pounds that is

00:08:01.495 --> 00:08:03.513
laying on a person or on top of a person,

00:08:03.513 --> 00:08:05.155
that can essentially just crush you.

00:08:05.155 --> 00:08:07.090
Your heart, your lungs, your kidneys,

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.571
these things are pretty fragile.

00:08:08.571 --> 00:08:11.130
Dogs, cats, snakes.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.883
These can cause a lot of injuries, also.

00:08:13.883 --> 00:08:16.310
A lot of the infections that can be

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:19.070
in, like, a cat's mouth
when they use their claws

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:22.730
to get inside of a joint,

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.848
this can cause a lot of
damage inside the joint.

00:08:24.848 --> 00:08:26.498
So as orthopedic surgeons,

00:08:26.498 --> 00:08:27.870
we would normally take this patient

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:31.359
to the operating room to
wash out their wounds,

00:08:31.359 --> 00:08:32.609
wash out their joints,

00:08:32.609 --> 00:08:34.833
give them antibiotics, tetanus.

00:08:34.833 --> 00:08:36.563
Most likely three out of 10.

00:08:38.478 --> 00:08:39.853
[slo-mo cracking]

00:08:39.853 --> 00:08:42.200
I like the extra added effect

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:45.035
that she landed right on
the barstool on her neck.

00:08:45.035 --> 00:08:47.350
You know, there's a lot of
injuries that you can sustain

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.630
to your eye, to your facial structures.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.794
You can actually receive what's
called a epidural hematoma,

00:08:53.794 --> 00:08:56.820
and these are blood
collections, hematomas,

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.740
that collect either on top of the dura,

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:02.810
which is the kind of hard
structure surrounding the brain,

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:04.290
or under the dura.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.480
It's called a subdural hematoma.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.610
And this, when there's
a collection of blood,

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.067
can cause a lot of pressure on the brain

00:09:11.067 --> 00:09:14.260
that, as you know, the
brain is responsible

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.300
for a lot of our functions, a
lot of our thinking, speech.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:18.998
So, it can affect those things.

00:09:18.998 --> 00:09:22.148
So, this is a injury that

00:09:22.148 --> 00:09:24.214
can be pretty fatal.

00:09:24.214 --> 00:09:27.270
There actually have been
lots of boxers who have died

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:31.800
from blows to their
heads, from these bleeds.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.970
You do have some vessels in your neck

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.060
called your vertebral arteries

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.360
that can be injured.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.160
You can have fractures in
your neck from that fall.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.660
You may not hear the bone
fracture in your neck,

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.530
but the bigger, larger bones,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.813
you'll hear that snap.

00:09:47.813 --> 00:09:51.215
[celery snapping]

00:09:51.215 --> 00:09:52.890
[bone snapping]

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.660
This scene is pretty realistic

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:58.460
in terms of the audio kind
of quality or the sounds.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.880
We hear these sounds a lot,

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:02.080
especially in the ER,
when a patient comes in

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.510
and let's say they dislocated their hip.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.643
Usually in car accidents,

00:10:06.643 --> 00:10:08.884
when their knees hit the dashboard

00:10:08.884 --> 00:10:11.529
and their hip dislocates,

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:15.240
it's usually called a
posterior hip dislocation.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.390
In the ER,

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.373
what we do as orthopedic surgeons,

00:10:18.373 --> 00:10:20.906
I will usually stand
on top of the patient,

00:10:20.906 --> 00:10:22.510
on top of their bed,

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.650
and actually pull on their leg

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.430
while someone is pushing down.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.413
And you can actually hear that hip

00:10:28.413 --> 00:10:30.022
just pop back in the socket.

00:10:30.022 --> 00:10:33.643
[popping]

00:10:33.643 --> 00:10:34.820
Chiropractor: And relax for me.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.970
Antonio: It's a very gratifying feeling

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.360
for orthopedic surgeons, especially me.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.440
I love those sounds,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.210
but other people may
cringe when they hear them.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:44.620
I would say the survival rate in this

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.000
is eight out of 10, seven out of 10,

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.900
depending on what type of injuries,

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.026
what happens in her brain

00:10:51.026 --> 00:10:53.560
to ensure that she doesn't
have any brain damage

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.583
or injuries in her brain
that could be deadly.

00:11:02.976 --> 00:11:04.109
[web stretching]

00:11:04.109 --> 00:11:05.610
[cracking]

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:09.520
The chance of someone
snapping their back like this

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:12.700
and surviving is pretty slim.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.450
The major vessels in your body,

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.890
your aorta and your inferior vena cava,

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:19.410
these things are pretty fragile.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.470
And when you take a lot of force

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.270
or when there's a lot of
force applied to the body,

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:27.238
it can rip these vessels pretty easily,

00:11:27.238 --> 00:11:29.530
especially from a height like this.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:31.500
When her body is snapped,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.943
that can actually tear and
cause injury to the spinal cord

00:11:35.943 --> 00:11:38.820
and the nerves that are
in the back as well.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.980
I would imagine if she
would have survived,

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.530
she most likely would have been paralyzed

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.780
or would have sustained
substantial damage.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:49.110
I'm a big Spider-Man fan.

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.765
I think a better kind of option,

00:11:51.765 --> 00:11:53.690
he didn't have many choices at this point,

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:55.120
would be to cast a net

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.230
and she fall into the net,

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:00.280
where just this one web didn't actually

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:03.160
just pull on her spine and
pull on her body like that,

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:05.100
and she just forcefully went back

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.840
and kinda snapped her back.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.266
This is a unsurvivable injury.

00:12:12.608 --> 00:12:14.541
[car brakes screeching]

00:12:14.541 --> 00:12:17.100
[crashing]

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.990
So, this is another kind of bad injury.

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.080
We call it a MVC rollover.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.800
These are pretty serious injuries,

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.240
and sometimes they can be quite fatal.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.920
He is rolling in the stretcher
through the hospital.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:32.168
He most likely would
have a cervical collar,

00:12:32.168 --> 00:12:36.370
which is a collar that is
applied by the EMS personnel

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:38.510
to stabilize the spine.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.500
He should have had a cervical collar on.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.100
They should have immobilized him

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.511
before they extricated him from the car.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.080
The seat belts are important
as well as the airbag.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:57.146
The airbag can actually cause injuries

00:12:57.146 --> 00:12:59.260
where it can fracture your ribs,

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.130
but it does protect you
from the steering wheel

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.628
as well as being ejected from the vehicle.

00:13:05.628 --> 00:13:07.460
So, you can have injuries to your thorax,

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:09.170
to your chest, to your abdomen.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:10.800
So a lot of these patients

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.660
are receiving multiple imaging studies

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:17.880
such as a CT scan to search
for a lot of these injuries

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.310
when they come into the emergency room.

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:23.140
It looks like he had
injuries to bilateral wrist.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.100
You can see the gauze

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.690
and the wrapping that he has on his wrist.

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:33.650
At that point, the chance of survival...

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:35.430
it looks like he's awake at this point.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.620
His eye is, the right eye is open.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.520
But the chance of survival,

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:40.712
six out of 10.

00:13:43.839 --> 00:13:44.672
[Johnny yells]

00:13:49.959 --> 00:13:51.460
[screaming]

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:55.016
How fast they were going from the sky

00:13:55.016 --> 00:13:56.310
to actually have landed on the ground,

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.080
it looks like they slowed
it down quite a bit,

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:01.430
but I've been skydiving before.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:03.857
I would have thought that the impact

00:14:03.857 --> 00:14:06.200
on the ground would have been greater.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.530
Usually patients, they
break their heel bone,

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.140
called your calcaneus.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.565
And when this happens, it actually just

00:14:12.565 --> 00:14:13.930
crushes the calcaneus,

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.860
crushes some other bones
that are in the feet.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.620
Depending on the type of land,

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.210
whether they're landing on their feet,

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.950
whether they're landing on
their hands or their back,

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.290
that will dictate what type
of injuries are sustained.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.380
The chance of them getting
up and walking is pretty low.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.160
A lot of times we have adrenaline,

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.340
our fight-or-flight response is activated

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:36.490
and you have this adrenaline

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.631
after accidents or falls,

00:14:38.631 --> 00:14:41.389
and you can still get up and do things,

00:14:41.389 --> 00:14:43.852
but you may be seriously injured.

00:14:43.852 --> 00:14:46.165
So, this was most likely

00:14:46.165 --> 00:14:47.911
what happened in this scene here.

00:14:47.911 --> 00:14:52.650
I would imagine kind of a
tuck-and-roll-type fall,

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:55.833
where you're protecting your
body and you're rolling,

00:14:55.833 --> 00:14:59.330
and hopefully that will
minimize some of the damage

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:00.780
or injuries that you sustain.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.040
This is a survivable injury

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.894
if it was based off of this movie scene,

00:15:05.894 --> 00:15:07.660
but if it was in real life

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:10.800
and he had a greater impact on the ground,

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.766
the chance of survival would have been

00:15:13.766 --> 00:15:15.221
six out of 10, maybe.

00:15:15.221 --> 00:15:17.130
[car brakes screech]
[crashing]

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.660
Oh, wow, bowling ball
just comes out of nowhere.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.442
Just hits him in the chest.

00:15:21.442 --> 00:15:26.210
This can be a pretty
serious injury as well.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:29.440
The injuries could be
anywhere from facial trauma

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.210
to lung bone injuries

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:34.980
to injuries to their soft
tissues, musculoskeletal.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.010
That bowling ball probably
broke a couple ribs

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.282
when it hit him in the chest right there.

00:15:40.282 --> 00:15:42.510
[cracking]

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:45.420
If someone did get hit in
the head with a bowling ball,

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.530
that's a pretty heavy ball.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:52.230
That would have caused a lot of injury

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.240
to the face itself, to the brain.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.945
There is really not much that kind of

00:15:57.945 --> 00:15:59.703
surprises me at this point.

00:15:59.703 --> 00:16:03.520
I had a patient come in
after a airplane crash.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.210
He had a small airplane and he took it up,

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:07.210
and it crashed for some reason.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.730
He had multiple fractures

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.670
and had a lot of other bad injuries,

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.907
but he was actually still talking to me

00:16:12.907 --> 00:16:15.660
when I saw him in the emergency room.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:19.841
We've had attacks by
different metal objects,

00:16:19.841 --> 00:16:24.717
brooms, gunshot wounds, explosions.

00:16:24.717 --> 00:16:25.560
I've seen it all.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:26.920
I've seen a lot of bad injuries.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:31.220
A gentleman who came in
who had leg amputations

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:33.360
from a motorcycle accident.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.010
His body was on the stretcher,

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:37.866
and his legs, both legs were
in the bag in the corner.

00:16:37.866 --> 00:16:42.335
So, that's a pretty typical day

00:16:42.335 --> 00:16:45.430
in the trauma emergency room.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:49.170
And as orthopedic surgeons, we
treat all of these patients.

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:51.760
Surviving a bowling
ball injury to the face

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:54.250
when it's going that fast is pretty slim.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:55.760
The areas that he landed on,

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.010
the tops of the roofs,

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:58.530
the things that he hit on the way down

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.460
may have braced him some,

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.353
but there's really no correct way to fall.

